{"kind":"report","text":"Review of task/fold-linear at 6d88b07eacac43ba8d91d22921b86e816357ddf0: APPROVED at this exact head. Head verified exact, worktree clean, scope is one commit touching fold.go and its tests. make vet, make test, make build, and go test -race ./... all pass. The change replaces a per-grant rescan of the live supersession set with a reference-counted retirement projection maintained as records land, plus role-grant indexing by actor-and-role and by membership statement. The structural question is that the position parameter disappears: authority checks used to be answered as of the deciding record's index, and now read current state. That is sound, and the reason is visible in append — a record's decision is taken before its own effects are applied, and effects are applied only when the verdict is effective, so at decision time the retirement and grant state reflects exactly the records before it, which is what retiredBefore(index) computed. Project-time calls previously passed the full length and now read the same final state. I did not rest on reading it. Three independent checks. First, a differential run: the old binary from main and the new one from this branch, folding the same live 167-event workroom, produced byte-identical projections of 277,337 bytes, and that history contains real supersession chains, withdrawn requests, ratifications and role grants. Second, equivalence on the cases the conditions name: I copied this branch's new retirement test into a detached worktree of main and ran it against the old fold, where it passes unchanged, so multiple superseders and nested resurrection behave identically before and after — this is a performance change, not a semantic one. The test itself is well-built, folding successive prefixes and asserting the retired state of the target as causes accumulate, are removed one at a time, and are restored through a supersession of a supersession. Third, the four existing authority tests covering roster supersession and resurrection, membership revocation of dependent authority, modern operator grants preserving agent kind and membership basis, and authority grants not substituting for membership, pass against both implementations, which is the legacy-and-modern grant semantics condition. On the benchmark, I reproduced it on both sides rather than only on the branch, at three actor counts. Old: 2.70 ms at 100 actors, 164.4 ms at 1000, and 4,023.6 ms at 5000. New: 1.65 ms, 13.1 ms, and 58.8 ms. Between 1000 and 5000 actors the old implementation grows 24.5-fold for a 5-fold input increase, an exponent near 2.0, while the new one grows 4.5-fold, an exponent near 0.93. So the branch does what it claims: 30,001 records fold in 59 milliseconds instead of four seconds, and the cost curve is linear rather than quadratic. The comment that documented the old quadratic shape as a known cost is correctly deleted rather than left to mislead. No findings. Approval is for 6d88b07eacac43ba8d91d22921b86e816357ddf0 only, any new commit requires a new review, and I have not merged anything.","body":{"benchmark":"old 2.70ms/164.4ms/4023.6ms vs new 1.65ms/13.1ms/58.8ms at 100/1000/5000 actors; growth exponent 2.0 -\u003e 0.93","equivalence":"old and new folds produce byte-identical 277337-byte projections on the live 167-event workroom; the new retirement test and four existing authority tests pass against the old fold unchanged","gates":"make vet, make test, make build, go test -race ./... all pass; head exact; worktree clean","head":"6d88b07eacac43ba8d91d22921b86e816357ddf0","result":"approved"}}